  "live_records": {
    "q-2523": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2523",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the function $y={x}^{(2x+1)}.$ Its derivative is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2x}\\cdot g(x).\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "We have the function \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2x+1}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2x+1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y={x}^{2x+1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x+1}(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}\\cdot x(\\frac{2x+1}{x}+\\frac{2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2x}(2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)=2x+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
